{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "control": "S"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 126, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Without a direct 0->3 edge, removing edge 5 leaves only the path through node 1 (edge 4), so the cell loses much more capacity or connectivity (cf. exp 61, -28 pts on C10)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "higher", "why": "A residual 0->3 skip keeps a guaranteed input-to-output path, so dropping edge 5 is far less damaging."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 4 already absent, removing edge 5 leaves at most the single 0->3 edge feeding the output, which collapses the cell's depth or disconnects it."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 5 is missing, the output relies on a single path, so adding a residual 0->3 skip adds a lot of gradient flow and diversity (cf. exp 22, +16.5 on IN16)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing a parameterized conv on 0->3 with a skip removes capacity, whereas replacing none or pooling only adds a residual (cf. exp 19, negative on all three datasets)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "direction": "higher", "why": "Swapping a weak 1x1 conv for an identity loses little capacity and improves optimization, unlike swapping out a 3x3 conv (exp 83-84 positive on all three datasets)."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing the strongest op, a 3x3 conv, with an identity removes the most useful computation, while replacing 1x1, pool or none costs little (exp 99 positive vs. 92/93 negative)."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a 3x3 conv where there was no edge adds a new path and capacity, while overwriting an existing skip or pool often disrupts a useful structure (exp 41-42 positive vs. 35-38 negative)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Filling an empty 1->3 edge with a conv adds a path, while replacing skip, 1x1 or pool trades away existing benefits (exp 71 vs. 72-74)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Turning an absent 2->3 edge into a 3x3 conv connects node 2 to the output and adds depth, a larger gain than upgrading an existing op (cf. exp 4, +45.7 on IN16)."}], "tok": 19176, "bad": 0, "probes": [[["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_1x1", "avg_pool_3x3", "skip_connect", "none", "nor_conv_1x1", "none"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_1x1", "avg_pool_3x3", "skip_connect", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_1x1", "none"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_1x1", "avg_pool_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_1x1", "nor_conv_1x1", "none"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["skip_connect", "nor_conv_1x1", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "none", "skip_connect"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["skip_connect", "nor_conv_1x1", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "none", "skip_connect"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["avg_pool_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_1x1", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["avg_pool_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_1x1", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [5, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3",